Order32 with fifteen hexagons per edge
======================================

The acquired lower bound leaves only order32 to exclude. For a root edge,
the two depth-two Moore trees have26 vertices and15 cross edges. Each side
has12 missing incidences. The remaining six vertices have total degree24,
so the exterior-edge identity forces no edge among them. Every remaining
vertex thus has exactly four neighbours among the Moore leaves.

At most one neighbour can belong to each of the three branches on either
side, since two such neighbours give a quadrangle. Thus every outside
vertex has between one and three neighbours on each side, whose counts
sum to four. Each side has12 incidences distributed over six vertices.
If a is the number with one neighbour on that side, it is also the number
with three, and6-2a have two. The opposite side has the same distribution.
Therefore each side contributes6+a pairs, with0<=a<=3.

The acquired opposite-root-pair identity equates this number with
(3*12 - sum(d_i^2))/2 on the other side. Consequently the two profile
values must agree and lie in[6,9]. This is a necessary root prune, not a
claim that the profile or the root ball determines a completion.

For complete coverage, use all retained root representatives, enumerate
all first-appearance side partitions into exactly six blocks with sizes
1..3, and all bijections between opposite blocks whose sizes sum to4.
Join each such pair and check girth and every edge's exact hexagon count.
No unseen vertices or extra edges are possible.
